{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-163007",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, the distance between a point and the line $y=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-3|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}k{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot [y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}(8-(-1))(3-0) \\\\  & =9k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2}=\\frac{M}{9}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{M}{9}{x}^{2}|y-3{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{M}{9}{x}^{2}(y-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}({\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{3}(y-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}(y-3)^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(8-(-1))\\cdot [\\frac{1}{3}(0+27)] \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}\\cdot 3\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
